Middle_Eastern/Harees"}, {"title": "bafybeiemxf5abjwjbikoz4mc3a3dla6ual3jsgpdr4cjr3oz3evfyavhwq.ipfs.4everland.io", "url": "https://bafybeiemxf5abjwjbikoz4mc3a3dla6ual3jsgpdr4cjr3oz3evfyavhwq.ipfs.4everland.io/wiki/Harees.html"}, {"title": "hellskitchen.206.189.43.227.sslip.io", "url": "https://hellskitchen.206.189.43.227.sslip.io/dish/1362"}]}, "The search didn't find any source that says what Harees is called in Zanzibar. The results only mention Zanzibar in unrelated contexts, such as Zanzibari mashed green bananas (Ndizi Mbichi) and a Swahili coconut shrimp curry (Kamba wa nazi).\n\nWhat the results do say about Harees in general:\n\n- It is known by many names, including Hareesa, Haleem, Arizah, Harisa, Jarish.\n- In East Africa, the results only cover Ethiopia, where Hareesa, or harees, is a popular dish mainly in the Harari region. It is cooked with lamb or beef and served on Eid or special occasions.\n- The results do not name a Swahili or Zanzibari term for the dish.\n\nA search on \"Swahili\" or \"Zanzibar\" plus \"porridge\" or \"uji\" might turn up a local name, if you want me to try again."], "durationSeconds": 6.158159374999999, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_016AWWrxB3UB53N4nnQvXcFq", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
